    {
      "id": "CEA-006",
      "category": "overstatement",
      "finding_summary": "The abstract, introduction and conclusion present the full-run counts (92 opening articles, 43 projects, 29 dated to day or month) as what the pipeline found, but only 34 pilot articles were checked against human coding. The pilot's one false positive among 31 not-relevant articles leaves precision on the 92 articles, and accuracy of the 29 dates, unestablished.",
      "issue_type": "manuscript_issue",
      "severity": "high",
      "confidence": "high",
      "location": {
        "page": 12,
        "page_label": "12",
        "section": "7 Conclusion",
        "text_quote": "43 unique projects. Of these, 29 can be dated to the day or month.",
        "precision": "exact"
      },
      "claim_text": "The pipeline was applied to 3,187 articles, identifying 43 unique projects, of which 29 can be dated to the day or month; the study demonstrated the potential for LLMs to construct dated records.",
      "assessment": "partially",
      "cannot_verify_reason": null,
      "evidence_summary": "Section 8 says 'a random sample from the full run of 3,187 articles should also be checked against human coding' and grouping into projects is a manual step. In the pilot, Table 4 has 1 LLM-relevant article among the 31 pilot-coder not-relevant articles (the Stord passing mention) and 3 true positives. No displayed check of the 92 relevant articles, the 43 projects, or the 29 dates exists. Table 6 does show internally consistent totals (19+10+9+5=43; 19+10=29). Table 6 also shows that 14 of 43 projects are dated only to year or not stated. The conclusion's 'demonstrated the potential' is hedged, but the abstract and conclusion state the counts as findings.",
